# DOM XSS Prevention

Semester files, user registries, theory libraries, and site libraries are **untrusted stored input**. Treat every dynamic string from JSON as potentially hostile.

## Required when writing or changing UI

1. **Never** put dynamic data into `innerHTML`, `insertAdjacentHTML`, or `showDialog(..., bodyHtml)` without escaping.
2. Use project helpers — do not invent quote-only escapes:
   - Text: `escapeHtml` from [`src/ui/dialogs.js`](src/ui/dialogs.js) or `escHtml` from [`src/ui/setup/dom-utils.js`](src/ui/setup/dom-utils.js)
   - Attributes: `escAttr` from `src/ui/setup/dom-utils.js` (escapes `& < > "`)
3. Prefer safe DOM APIs for text-only UI: `textContent`, `createElement('option')` + `.value` / `.textContent`.
4. **No `innerHTML +=` rebuild loops.** Build a string (or DOM nodes), then assign once. `innerHTML +=` re-parses existing DOM text as HTML and triggers CodeQL `js/xss-through-dom`.
5. When reviewing diffs, grep for `innerHTML` and confirm every interpolated value is escaped.

## Examples

Bad:

```javascript
select.innerHTML += '<option value="' + s.id + '">' + s.name + '</option>';
el.innerHTML = '<span title="' + group + '">' + label + '</span>';
```

Good:

```javascript
var opt = document.createElement('option');
opt.value = s.id;
opt.textContent = s.name;
select.appendChild(opt);

// or, when HTML strings are required:
el.innerHTML = '<span title="' + escAttr(group) + '">' + escapeHtml(label) + '</span>';
```

## Scope note

Static literals and intentional trusted HTML (e.g. calendar week headers from `getWeekDisplay(..., false)`) are fine. Student names, group ids, facility names, proposal text, file names, and course codes are not trusted.
